class Solution {
public:
    int numJewelsInStones(string jewels, string stones) {
        unordered_set<char>jewels_type;
        for(char c : jewels){
            jewels_type.insert(c);
        }
        int count = 0;
        for(int i=0; i<stones.length(); i++){
            if(jewels_type.find(stones[i]) != jewels_type.end()){
                count++;
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
};

review.raw:
VERDICT
Correct: your code correctly stores all jewel characters in a hash set and checks each stone against the set in optimal linear time.

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well:

You chose the ideal data structure (unordered_set) for average constant-time membership lookups.

You initialized your counter to 0 and incremented it accurately.

You used the standard find and end iterator comparison to test presence.

Is it correct?

Yes, it is fully correct and passes all test cases on LeetCode.

Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses:

There are no logical bugs.

You can write your set insertion much more cleanly by passing the string iterators directly to the constructor: unordered_set jewel_set(jewels.begin(), jewels.end());.

Using range-based for loops for stones (for (char s : stones)) is cleaner and avoids comparing signed int i with unsigned stones.length().

You can use jewels_type.count(stones[i]) instead of jewels_type.find(...) != jewels_type.end() for shorter, cleaner code.

Edge cases handled properly: jewels and stones having no characters in common (returns 0), all stones being jewels (returns stones length), single-character strings, and case sensitivity where 'a' and 'A' are treated as distinct jewels.

Time and space complexity of MY code, each with a one-line reason:

Time complexity: O(J + S) where J is jewels.length() and S is stones.length(), because inserting J characters and checking S characters each take average O(1) time.

Space complexity: O(J) auxiliary space because the unordered_set stores up to J unique jewel characters.

Why my approach works:

Imagine a bouncer at a club who holds a VIP list (jewels). Instead of asking every person in the queue to check with everyone in VIP, the bouncer memorizes the VIP names in a notepad (the hash set). For each person in line (stone), the bouncer looks at the notepad in one step and clicks the counter if they are on it. For jewels = "aA", stones = "aAAbbbb": the set stores {'a', 'A'}. Reading stones gives: 'a' (yes, +1), 'A' (yes, +1), 'A' (yes, +1), 'b' (no), 'b' (no), 'b' (no), 'b' (no). Total count is 3.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: Brute Force (Nested Loops)

Where it comes from:

Standard naive baseline mentioned in the LeetCode editorial and introductory programming lessons.

Idea:

For every single character in stones, loop through all characters of jewels to see if there is a match. If found, increment the count and stop checking that stone.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <string>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int numJewelsInStones(string jewels, string stones) {
        int count = 0;
        for (char s : stones) {
            for (char j : jewels) {
                if (s == j) {
                    count++;
                    break;
                }
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(J * S) where J is jewels.length() and S is stones.length(), because for each stone we scan the jewels string.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space because it uses only a single integer counter.

What makes the next approach better:

Instead of scanning jewels from start to finish for every stone, we can sort jewels and use binary search to test membership in logarithmic time.

Approach 2: Sorting and Binary Search

Where it comes from:

Classic divide-and-conquer search pattern common in competitive programming.

Idea:

Sort the jewels string alphabetically. For each character in stones, perform a binary search (using std::binary_search) to see if it exists in jewels.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int numJewelsInStones(string jewels, string stones) {
        sort(jewels.begin(), jewels.end());
        int count = 0;
        for (char s : stones) {
            if (binary_search(jewels.begin(), jewels.end(), s)) {
                count++;
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(J log J + S log J) to sort jewels and perform S binary searches.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space beyond the sorting stack frame.

What makes the next approach better:

Binary search takes O(log J) per stone. A hash set can verify membership in average O(1) time without needing sorting.

Approach 3: Hash Set (Your Approach Refactored)

Where it comes from:

Official LeetCode editorial Approach 2 and standard hash-based lookup patterns.

Idea:

Put all characters of jewels into an unordered_set. Then iterate through stones, checking if each character exists in the set.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <string>
#include <unordered_set>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int numJewelsInStones(string jewels, string stones) {
        unordered_set<char> jewelSet(jewels.begin(), jewels.end());
        int count = 0;
        for (char s : stones) {
            if (jewelSet.count(s)) {
                count++;
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(J + S) average time because set insertion and set lookup run in O(1) on average.

Space Complexity:

O(J) auxiliary space to store unique jewels in the hash set.

What makes the next approach better:

A hash set has hashing overhead, dynamically allocates heap memory for buckets/nodes, and can degrade to O(J) worst-case under hash collisions. Because characters are ASCII letters, we can use a fixed-size direct-address boolean array or bitset for guaranteed O(1) worst-case time and O(1) space.

Approach 4: Fixed-Size Boolean Lookup Array / Direct Addressing

Where it comes from:

Top-voted LeetCode discussion solutions and GeeksforGeeks character mapping tutorials.

Idea:

English letters (lowercase and uppercase) have ASCII values from 65 ('A') to 122 ('z'). We can use an array of size 128 (or 256) initialized to false. Mark array[j] = true for every character in jewels, then increment count whenever array[s] is true for a stone.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <string>
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int numJewelsInStones(string jewels, string stones) {
        bool isJewel[128] = {false};
        for (char j : jewels) {
            isJewel[static_cast<unsigned char>(j)] = true;
        }
        int count = 0;
        for (char s : stones) {
            if (isJewel[static_cast<unsigned char>(s)]) {
                count++;
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(J + S) strictly linear with zero hashing overhead.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space because the array size is a constant 128 bytes regardless of input string length.

What makes the next approach better:

Instead of using 128 bytes of memory, we can store 128 flags inside a 64-bit integer bitmask (or std::bitset<128>) to use only 8 to 16 bytes of memory with CPU bitwise operations.

Approach 5: Bit Manipulation / 64-bit Bitmask

Where it comes from:

Low-level competitive programming and micro-optimization community posts.

Idea:

The problem constraints specify that jewels and stones consist only of English letters ('A'-'Z' and 'a'-'z'). 'A' is 65 and 'z' is 122. The difference 122 - 65 + 1 is 58, which fits inside a single 64-bit integer (unsigned long long has 64 bits). Set bit (c - 'A') for each jewel, and test bit (s - 'A') for each stone.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <string>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int numJewelsInStones(string jewels, string stones) {
        unsigned long long mask = 0;
        for (char j : jewels) {
            mask |= (1ULL << (j - 'A'));
        }
        int count = 0;
        for (char s : stones) {
            if ((mask >> (s - 'A')) & 1ULL) {
                count++;
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(J + S) deterministic linear time running purely on CPU registers.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space using a single 8-byte integer.

WHERE I STAND

Which approach did I use?

You used Approach 3 (Hash Set).

Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem?

Interviewers primarily expect Approach 3 (Hash Set) because it directly checks your understanding of hash structures, membership checking, and trade-offs. However, bringing up Approach 4 (a fixed-size array of 128 or 256 booleans) or Approach 5 earns bonus points for spotting character set constraints and eliminating dynamic allocation overhead.

Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:

Question 1: What is the worst-case time complexity of using unordered_set?

Answer: In the worst case where every character produces a hash collision, lookups take O(J) time instead of O(1), making total time O(J + S * J). Mention that this is why a direct 128-boolean array or bitmask is preferred when characters are bounded ASCII.

Question 2: What if the characters are arbitrary Unicode characters (UTF-8/UTF-32) instead of English letters?

Answer: An ASCII table or 64-bit bitmask won't work because Unicode has over 140,000 characters. In that case, std::unordered_set<char32_t> is the best approach because it handles sparse, large key spaces efficiently without wasting memory on huge sparse arrays.

Question 3: What if stones is a huge stream of characters that does not fit in memory?

Answer: Keep the jewels set in memory (since jewels is small, at most 50 characters). Read the stones stream chunk by chunk, checking each incoming character against the jewels set and accumulating the count.

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER (for my revision notes)

The key insight, in one sentence:

Converting an inner search loop into an O(1) lookup table (using a hash set or fixed boolean array) turns an O(J * S) brute force solution into optimal O(J + S) linear time.

My mistakes, as a short checklist for next time:

Use container range constructors like unordered_set set(str.begin(), str.end()) instead of manual insert loops.

Prefer jewel_set.count(c) over jewel_set.find(c) != jewel_set.end() for concise boolean existence checks.

Prefer range-based for loops (for (char c : str)) over indexed loops when the index is not needed.

When inputs consist only of ASCII characters, always consider a fixed array of size 128/256 for zero-overhead O(1) lookups.

The pattern or idea this question is really about:

Membership Checking and Set Lookups (Trading Space for Time).

